    {
      "id": "challenge",
      "name": "06  CHALLENGES",
      "width": 1460,
      "height": 950,
      "controls": [
        {
          "id": "challengeHeader",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "06  INVESTIGATE  /  THINK LIKE AN ENGINEER",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 12,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 55,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": false,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "goalOne",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "CHALLENGE A  •  Discover the cutoff",
          "x": 25,
          "y": 85,
          "width": 690,
          "height": 179,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Run a MODEL sweep, then a physical sweep on the same circuit. Find the frequency where the output amplitude falls to 0.707 of Vin (low-pass), or rises to 0.707 (high-pass). Compare with fC=1/(2πRC). Why are the values not identical?",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "goalTwo",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "CHALLENGE B  •  Phase leads or lags?",
          "x": 739,
          "y": 85,
          "width": 697,
          "height": 179,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "At fC the low-pass output should lag by 45°. The high-pass output should lead by 45°. Explain which physical quantity in the capacitor causes this. Examine phase(CH2) − phase(CH1), not the absolute trigger time.",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "goalThree",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "CHALLENGE C  •  Investigate tolerances",
          "x": 25,
          "y": 281,
          "width": 690,
          "height": 177,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Swap 100 nF for a measured capacitor, e.g. 91 nF or 110 nF, and record the actual value in the SIMULATE parameter. Repeat the sweep. Does the predicted cutoff move in the correct direction? Keep the model and real circuit component values synchronized.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "goalFour",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "CHALLENGE D  •  Ask better questions",
          "x": 739,
          "y": 281,
          "width": 697,
          "height": 177,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "What happens if you use longer ground leads? Does the 1 MΩ oscilloscope input load the 1 kΩ node? What happens near the noise floor? Does the result change when you sample more cycles? Are your fitted amplitudes consistent with RMS?",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "eqLP",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "Low-pass transfer",
          "x": 25,
          "y": 476,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 109,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "H_{LP}(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{1+j\\omega RC}",
            "pointSize": 22,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "eqHP",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "High-pass transfer",
          "x": 739,
          "y": 476,
          "width": 697,
          "height": 109,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "H_{HP}(j\\omega)=\\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC}",
            "pointSize": 22,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "eqFc",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "Cutoff and time constant",
          "x": 25,
          "y": 600,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 100,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "f_c=\\frac{1}{2\\pi RC}\\qquad \\tau=RC",
            "pointSize": 22,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "eqDb",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "From amplitude ratio to decibels",
          "x": 739,
          "y": 600,
          "width": 697,
          "height": 100,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "G_{dB}=20\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}\\right)",
            "pointSize": 22,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "challengeCompute",
          "type": "button",
          "text": "REVEAL CURRENT MODEL PREDICTION",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 716,
          "width": 690,
          "height": 57,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {
            "clicked": {
              "handler": "onQuickQuestionClicked",
              "enabled": true
            }
          },
          "properties": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "challengeAnswer",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "Choose a frequency on SIMULATE, then reveal its calculated response and explain the result.",
          "x": 739,
          "y": 716,
          "width": 696,
          "height": 62,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "studentDeliverables",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "SUGGESTED LAB REPORT",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 797,
          "width": 1412,
          "height": 131,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Include (1) a breadboard photo, (2) your circuit and installed component values, (3) one CH1/CH2 capture, (4) a gain/phase sweep exported from Notebook, (5) a comparison to theory and tolerances, and (6) an explanation of any anomalous measurements. Your `.wfpanel` carries the interactive lesson, but exported data and your photos are separate files. Add Page 09 CH1/CH2 residual RMS and discuss how noise and harmonics affect the fitted model.",
            "tone": "Success"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fit_theory",
      "name": "07  LEAST-SQUARES THEORY",
      "width": 1460,
      "height": 1065,
      "controls": [
        {
          "id": "fitTheoryHeader",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "07   WHY LEAST-SQUARES SINUSOID FITTING WORKS",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 12,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 55,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": false,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheoryIntro",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "A sine wave has a known frequency but unknown DC offset, peak amplitude and phase. Least squares estimates all three from every captured sample, including non-integer numbers of cycles.",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 80,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 70,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheory1",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "01  THE PHYSICAL PROBLEM",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 165,
          "width": 453,
          "height": 168,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Two AD3 inputs see the same excitation frequency. The input and output can have different amplitudes, phase angles, and DC offsets. Reading one peak or zero crossing would waste most samples and be sensitive to noise.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheory2",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "02  TURN PHASE INTO LINEAR COEFFICIENTS",
          "x": 502,
          "y": 165,
          "width": 453,
          "height": 168,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Write A sin(ωt+φ) = a sin(ωt) + b cos(ωt). Then a=A cosφ and b=A sinφ. Once the test frequency f is known, the basis sine and cosine values are fixed numbers at every sample.",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheory3",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "03  WHY THE SAMPLE RATE MATTERS",
          "x": 982,
          "y": 165,
          "width": 453,
          "height": 168,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "For sample i, t=i/fs and θ=2πfi/fs. We use the ACTUAL Scope sample rate, not merely the rate we requested. Both channels share the same sample time origin; subtracting phase angles removes that origin.",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheoryEq1",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "A three-parameter linear model",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 347,
          "width": 1413,
          "height": 104,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "v_i=d+a\\sin(2\\pi f i/f_s)+b\\cos(2\\pi f i/f_s)+e_i",
            "pointSize": 19,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheory4",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "04  WHAT DOES BEST FIT MEAN?",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 467,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 190,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Find d, a and b to minimize SSE = Σ[y[i] − (d+a s[i]+b c[i])]². Differentiate SSE with respect to each coefficient and set each derivative to zero. This produces the normal equations XᵀXβ = Xᵀy.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheory5",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "05  REMOVE THE DC OFFSET",
          "x": 730,
          "y": 467,
          "width": 705,
          "height": 190,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Center the sine, cosine and sample vectors by subtracting their means. The 3-unknown system reduces to two equations for a and b. In the code the centered matrix entries are named a, b, d (matrix terms); rhsS and rhsC are the right-hand sides.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheoryEq2",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "Two scalar equations (the 2 × 2 normal system)",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 670,
          "width": 1413,
          "height": 126,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "S_{ss}a+S_{sc}b=S_{sy}\\qquad S_{sc}a+S_{cc}b=S_{cy}",
            "pointSize": 18,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheory6",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "06  RECOVER THE MEASUREMENT",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 810,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 156,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "A = √(a²+b²), φ = atan2(b,a). Apply independently to CH1 and CH2. Gain = Aout/Ain; dB = 20 log10(gain); phase shift = φout−φin, wrapped to −180°..180°.",
            "tone": "Success"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheory7",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "07  CHECK HOW WELL THE MODEL FITS",
          "x": 730,
          "y": 810,
          "width": 705,
          "height": 156,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Reconstruct every sample as yhat[i]=d+a s[i]+b c[i]. The residual is e[i]=y[i]−yhat[i]; residual RMS=√(Σe²/N). The residual combines noise, harmonics, frequency error and other model mismatch. It is NOT a calibrated uncertainty.",
            "tone": "Warning"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitTheoryFoot",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "NEXT → Page 08 maps each equation to actual JavaScript source. Page 09 compares raw points, fitted waves and residuals on synthetic data or a REAL AD3 capture.",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 980,
          "width": 1413,
          "height": 74,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fit_source",
      "name": "08  SOURCE CODE WALKTHROUGH",
      "width": 1460,
      "height": 1070,
      "controls": [
        {
          "id": "fitCodeHeader",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "08   READ THE ACTUAL JAVASCRIPT ALGORITHM",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 12,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 55,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": false,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitCodeIntro",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "Open the Script Editor → embedded libraries → CaptureAnalysis.mjs. These are the same expressions used for physical measurements, not a replacement algorithm.",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 78,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 70,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitCode1",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 1 — BASIS & SAMPLE SUMS  /  fitSinusoid()",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 164,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 188,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "step = 2*Math.PI*f/rate;\ns = Math.sin(step*i); c = Math.cos(step*i);\nAccumulate s0, c0, ss, cc, sc, y0, ys, yc.\nThese are the XᵀX matrix and Xᵀy vector, built without keeping a huge matrix.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitCode2",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 2 — CENTER THE BASIS  /  fitSinusoid()",
          "x": 730,
          "y": 164,
          "width": 705,
          "height": 188,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "a = ss - s0*s0/n0;\nb = sc - s0*c0/n0;\nd = cc - c0*c0/n0;\nrhsS = ys - s0*y0/n0;\nrhsC = yc - c0*y0/n0;",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitCode3",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 3 — SOLVE TWO EQUATIONS",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 368,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 189,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "det = a*d - b*b;\nsinCoef = (rhsS*d - rhsC*b)/det;\ncosCoef = (rhsC*a - rhsS*b)/det;\noffset = (y0 - sinCoef*s0 - cosCoef*c0)/n0;\nSmall determinant? The fit is not identifiable and throws an error.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitCode4",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 4 — TURN COEFFICIENTS INTO A PHASOR",
          "x": 730,
          "y": 368,
          "width": 705,
          "height": 189,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "amplitude = Math.hypot(sinCoef,cosCoef);\nangle = Math.atan2(cosCoef,sinCoef);\nGiven y = d + a sinθ + b cosθ, atan2(b,a) is the PHASE in the sine convention. The argument order matters!",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitCode5",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 5 — COMPARE TWO CHANNELS",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 573,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 188,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "A = fitSinusoid(CH1,rate,f);\nB = fitSinusoid(CH2,rate,f);\ngain = B.amplitude / A.amplitude;\nphase = (B.angle-A.angle)*180/Math.PI;\nThe project wraps phase into (−180°,180°].",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitCode6",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 6 — INSPECT THE RESIDUAL",
          "x": 730,
          "y": 573,
          "width": 705,
          "height": 188,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "fitted = offset + sinCoef*sin(step*i) + cosCoef*cos(step*i);\nresidual = y - fitted;\nresidualSSE += residual*residual;\nrmsResidualV = Math.sqrt(residualSSE/n);\nPlot residual and review RMS, percent-of-peak and R².",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitCodeEq",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "From model parameters to voltage gain and phase",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 778,
          "width": 1413,
          "height": 113,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "|H|=\\frac{A_{out}}{A_{in}}\\qquad \\phi_H=\\phi_{out}-\\phi_{in}",
            "pointSize": 21,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitCode7",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "WHY NOT JUST TAKE AN FFT?",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 905,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 150,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "A Fourier method can estimate the same fundamental, but its handling of offset, windowing and fractional cycles matters. Least squares fits the exact commanded-frequency basis and DC term directly. This is related to synchronous/lock-in detection; it is NOT blind frequency estimation.",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "fitCode8",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "LIMITS TO REMEMBER",
          "x": 730,
          "y": 905,
          "width": 705,
          "height": 150,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "A small residual is not proof of calibration or absolute accuracy. The code assumes a stable single-frequency excitation, no missing samples and a common time base. Inspect Scope range, timing, settling and wiring; cross-check against WaveForms.",
            "tone": "Warning"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
